From 596e8653c38d63b182ede14f9d611fa778734201 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: khj1222 <39723594+khj1222@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 00:54:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] patch_cache: notice a label that changed under the same path

A cached split is keyed by the label paths it was found under. That catches
pointing a run at a different tree and misses regenerating a mask in place: the
cache is reused against labels it no longer describes, and nothing looks wrong,
because the patch count and the bounding boxes are the old ones. Training then
continues over supervision that has been deleted.

Fingerprint each label asset by the relative names and sizes of its files, and
carry that in the record and in the lookup key, so the rejection path that
already exists does the work -- no new invalidation logic. Rewriting a zarr
changes chunk sizes, and dropping annotation deletes chunks outright when the
store does not write empty ones, so the digest moves for the changes that matter.

It reads no chunk contents. On this repository's real w00 label assets, 12,619
files across the ink labels and the supervision mask, the fingerprint costs
22.5-32.4 ms over five runs; the same thing built on Path.stat() costs
187.4-270.5 ms, because os.scandir carries the size along on Windows and Linux
alike.

Two limits, deliberate: two different labels that compress to identical sizes
under identical names are indistinguishable, and a byte-identical copy of a tree
fingerprints the same as its source, which is the wanted behaviour. A cache
written before this change has no fingerprint, so it misses once and is rebuilt.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
